{"id":"tha-t0-0001","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"สวัสดี","text_romanized":"sa-wat-dii","text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0002","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"เช้า","text_romanized":"cháo","text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"morning","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0003","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"บ่าย","text_romanized":"bàai","text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"afternoon","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0004","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"เย็น","text_romanized":"yen","text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"evening","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0005","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"สบายดีหรือ","text_romanized":"sabaai-dii rue?","text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0006","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"ยินดีที่ได้รู้จัก","text_romanized":"yin-dii thii dai ruu-jak","text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0007","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"คุณชื่ออะไร","text_romanized":"khun chue arai?","text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What is your name?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0008","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.please","domain":1,"text_native":"กรุณา","text_romanized":"karunaa","text_english":"Please.","source_caption":"Please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Not a filler in English, and a grammatical particle in some of these languages — one word that changes a sentence from an order to a request.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0009","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"ขอบคุณ[มาก]","text_romanized":"khop khun [mâak]","text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0010","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"ไม่เป็นไร","text_romanized":"mai pen rai","text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0011","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"ขอโทษ","text_romanized":"kho thot","text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me. (begging pardon)","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0012","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"ลาก่อน","text_romanized":"laa kon","text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0013","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"ไม่เข้าใจ","text_romanized":"mai khao jai","text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0014","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"repair.speak-slower","domain":12,"text_native":"พูดช้าๆ หน่อย","text_romanized":"phûut cháa cháa nòi","text_english":"Please speak more slowly.","source_caption":"Please speak more slowly.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Asks for the thing you actually need. Saying it is nearly always received well, even badly pronounced, and asking again is not.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0015","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"repair.how-do-you-say","domain":12,"text_native":"พูดเป็นภาษาไทยอย่างไร","text_romanized":"phûut pen phaa-săa thai yàang-rai","text_english":"How do you say this?","source_caption":"How do you say this in Thai?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you learn a word instead of nodding. Asking is how a phrasebook gets used instead of recited.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0016","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-know","domain":12,"text_native":"ไม่ทราบ / ไม่รู้","text_romanized":"mâi sâap","text_english":"I do not know.","source_caption":"I don’t know.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The honest answer, and in some cultures the only acceptable one — guessing when you don't know reads as deceit rather than as effort.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0017","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"พูดภาษาอังกฤษได้ไหม","text_romanized":"phuut phaasaa ang-krit dai mai?","text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tha-t0-0018","lang":"tha","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.i-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"เข้าใจ","text_romanized":"kâo jai","text_english":"I understand.","source_caption":"I understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Saying it when you do not is worse than silence, because it removes the reason to simplify. Said when you do, it invites the harder version.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Thai phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thai_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
